@page "/accounts/roles"
@using JiranisokoTech.Application.Authorization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = PermissionClaim.PolicyPrefix + Permissions.RolesManage)]

@*
    The role matrix, shown and not edited — and that is the whole design.

    The matrix lives in one file in code, which is what lets the seeder, the
    policies, the tests and this page all read the same thing. A screen that
    edited it would mean the database could disagree with the code, and the
    disagreement would be invisible: the tests would still pass, because they
    test the code.

    So roles.manage opens this rather than a set of checkboxes. What it buys is
    the ability to answer "why can Charity do that" without reading source,
    which is the question this page actually gets asked.
*@

<PageTitle>Roles — Jiranisoko Delivery</PageTitle>

<nav class="crumbs"><a href="/accounts">Accounts</a></nav>

<div class="page-head">
    <h1>Roles</h1>
    <p class="subtle">@Roles.All.Count roles, @Permissions.All.Count permissions.</p>
</div>

<p>
    Roles are additive: somebody may hold several, and what they can do is the
    union. <strong>Interviewer</strong> is meant to be worn alongside another
    role rather than on its own.
</p>

<p class="subtle">
    This is read from the code that enforces it, not from the database — so what
    is on this page is what actually applies. Changing it is a change to
    <code>Permissions.cs</code>, deliberately: a matrix editable at runtime is
    one the tests can no longer vouch for.
</p>

@foreach (var group in Grouped)
{
    <section class="standing">
        <h2>@group.Key</h2>

        <table class="grid">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">@group.Key permissions by role</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Permission</th>
                    @foreach (var role in Roles.All)
                    {
                        <th scope="col">@Name(role)</th>
                    }
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var permission in group)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>@permission</code></td>
                        @foreach (var role in Roles.All)
                        {
                            <td class="number">
                                @if (Roles.PermissionsFor(role).Contains(permission))
                                {
                                    <span aria-label="yes">●</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span class="subtle" aria-label="no">—</span>
                                }
                            </td>
                        }
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Grouped by the noun in front of the dot.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Which is the grouping the naming convention was chosen for:
    /// resource.action means the resource is already the first thing on every
    /// line, and a table of sixty rows is unreadable without it.
    /// </remarks>
    private static IEnumerable<IGrouping<string, string>> Grouped =>
        Permissions.All.GroupBy(permission => permission.Split('.')[0]);

    private static string Name(string role) => role switch
    {
        Roles.Owner => "Owner",
        Roles.Administrator => "Admin",
        Roles.HumanResources => "HR",
        Roles.DepartmentHead => "Head",
        Roles.ProjectManager => "Manager",
        Roles.Developer => "Developer",
        Roles.Interviewer => "Interviewer",
        _ => role,
    };
}
